{"grade":6,"complete":false,"main_gap":"The converse contains an incorrect substitution in the middle term; replacing \(2C\) by \(C\) immediately repairs it.","errors":[{"description":"For \(f(x)=x+C\), the middle term is ​\((x+y+C)/2\), not \((x+y+2C)/2\). Thus the displayed chain is not the original one, though the corrected chain is exactly RMS–AM–GM for \(x\) and \(y+C\).","quote":"the desired chain becomes
\[
\sqrt{\frac{x^2+(y+C)^2}{2}}
\ge \frac{x+y+2C}2
\ge \sqrt{x(y+C)},
\]"}],"notes":"The necessity argument is rigorous: the iterate law forces nonnegative displacement, the quadratic estimate is correct, and subdivision proves the displacement constant. Only the readily repairable converse typo prevents full correctness as written."}
